From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] input: Adjustable scroll speed for mice and touchpads

libinput has no scroll speed option and Mutter never offered one, so a
wheel or touchpad that scrolls too far or too little could not be
changed. Scroll now scales per device where libinput hands it over,
before any client sees it, so every Wayland and Xwayland client and the
Shell itself follow the same speed. This is the approach KWin takes
with its per-device scroll factor.

Two settings, org.projectluma.peripherals.mouse scroll-speed and
org.projectluma.peripherals.touchpad scroll-speed (0.25 to 4.0, default
1.0), follow the same split as natural-scroll: touchpads use the
touchpad setting, every other pointer the mouse one. They apply live.

Finger and continuous deltas are multiplied, so a client's kinetic
scrolling starts from a proportionally faster or slower gesture and the
zero delta ending a gesture stays zero. High-resolution wheel values
are multiplied too, with the fraction too small to send kept per device
until it adds up, so the slowest speed still scrolls a step every four
detents; a reversal drops what was left over. A faster speed delivers
one detent per event, so clients counting wheel steps see them all.

At 1.0 no scaling code runs: both paths call the unchanged upstream
functions with the values libinput gave.
